{
  "abstract": "Three theorems about binary channels on a doubly symmetric binary source: a pair (𝑋, 𝑌) of\nBernoulli(1/2) bits with P(𝑋 ≠ 𝑌) = 𝑝, together with Markov chains 𝑈 - 𝑋 - 𝑌 - 𝑉 in which 𝑈\nand 𝑉 are the outputs of two independent binary channels applied to 𝑋 and to 𝑌. Let 𝒜 be the\nset of rate triples (𝑅₀, 𝑅₁, 𝑅₂) with 𝐼(𝑈;𝑋) ≤ 𝑅₁, 𝐼(𝑌;𝑉) ≤ 𝑅₂ and 𝑅₀ ≤ 𝐼(𝑈;𝑉) attainable by\narbitrary binary channels on the two sides, and ℬ the same set with both channels restricted\nto be binary symmetric.\n\n(1) conv 𝒜 = conv ℬ for every 𝑝 in [0,1]. (2) At 𝑝 = 0, no pair of binary channels whose rates\ndo not exceed those of the Z-channel with parameter 𝑎 and the S-channel with parameter 𝑑\nattains a larger 𝐼(𝑈;𝑉) than that pair does. (3) At 𝑝 = 0, no pair of binary channels whose\nrates are at least those of the Z-channels with parameters 𝑎 and 𝑑 attains a smaller 𝐼(𝑈;𝑉)\nthan that pair does. Here 𝑎, 𝑑 range over (0,1).",
